<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Massangano</h1><p class="st-meta">1850 CE – 1888 CE (approx.) · capital Massangano</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Da Cruz family's fortified state on the Zambezi, which repeatedly defeated Portuguese armies in the 19th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/da-cruz-family">Da Cruz family</a>, Afro-Portuguese prazo holders, built their stronghold at Massangano where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luenha-river">Luenha</a> joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a>, above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tete">Tete</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-vicente-da-cruz">António Vicente da Cruz</a>, known as Bonga, it became an independent power controlling the river route in the mid-19th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From a large aringa, a stockaded fort, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/da-cruz-family">Da Cruz</a> rulers commanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chikunda">Chikunda</a> soldiers, taxed river traffic and raided for slaves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>. They mixed Portuguese and African forms of authority and relied on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spirit-medium">spirit mediums</a> as well as firearms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-vicente-da-cruz">Bonga</a> destroyed four Portuguese expeditions between 1867 and 1869, an embarrassment to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-vicente-da-cruz">Bonga</a>'s death in 1879 his successors continued to resist, but a Portuguese force supported by rival warlords such as those of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/makanga">Makanga</a> captured Massangano in 1888, and the region came under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-mozambique">Portuguese Mozambique</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1850 CE – 1888 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.52%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1850 CE – 1888 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Massangano</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>António Vicente da Cruz (Bonga)</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chikunda, Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Mozambique</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Da Cruz state, Bonga's state</dd></dl></aside></div>

